From d451e3a25d09b2c6837f2793c5e1bac32b83d528 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stephen Smalley <stephen.smalley.work@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Oct 2024 10:17:11 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 78/90] selinux: annotate selinuxfs permission checks

Annotate the selinuxfs permission checks with comments
explaining why we only check permissions against the
current SELinux namespace (because these operations only
read or modify the state of the current SELinux namespace).
If we were instead to check permissions against ancestor
namespaces, we would need to be allowed by the ancestor
policies to perform the same operation in those namespaces,
which would be undesirable.

Signed-off-by: Stephen Smalley <stephen.smalley.work@gmail.com>

Upstream-commit: 056773a5e1db0daa19bd9c148fe72965161bb597
---
 security/selinux/selinuxfs.c | 56 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 55 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/security/selinux/selinuxfs.c b/security/selinux/selinuxfs.c
index be4a8ab401cefdb42a8ddacc26107548ca9af299..da65532e02218ae93f3c460c7febbcb0adf8d88a 100644
--- a/security/selinux/selinuxfs.c
+++ b/security/selinux/selinuxfs.c
@@ -167,6 +167,10 @@ static ssize_t sel_write_enforce(struct file *file, const char __user *buf,
 
 	old_value = enforcing_enabled(state);
 	if (new_value != old_value) {
+		/*
+		 * Only check against the current namespace because
+		 * this operation only affects it and no others.
+		 */
 		length = avc_has_perm(current_selinux_state,
 				      current_sid(), SECINITSID_SECURITY,
 				      SECCLASS_SECURITY, SECURITY__SETENFORCE,
@@ -488,6 +492,10 @@ static int sel_open_policy(struct inode *inode, struct file *filp)
